WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.827
One of the most important ideas in data cleaning is that an invalid value does not automatically tell us what the

00:00:08.827 --> 00:00:12.732
correct value should be. Suppose a product price is negative fifty dollars.

00:00:12.732 --> 00:00:19.782
We can easily use an absolute value function and turn negative fifty into positive fifty.

00:00:19.782 --> 00:00:26.752
Technically, that is easy. But do we actually know that fifty dollars is the correct price?

00:00:26.752 --> 00:00:30.072
Maybe the negative number represents a refund.

00:00:30.072 --> 00:00:32.752
Maybe the source system has an error.

00:00:32.752 --> 00:00:35.557
Maybe the intended value was five hundred.

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:42.902
So the safer response is to flag the value as invalid and investigate the source or the business rule.

00:00:42.902 --> 00:00:50.572
This distinction is important. Data cleaning should reduce inconsistency without inventing facts.

00:00:50.572 --> 00:00:54.932
Whenever you are tempted to automatically fix a suspicious value, ask yourself one question:

00:00:54.932 --> 00:01:02.496
do I have evidence for the replacement, or am I only choosing something that looks reasonable?
